{
  "dom_id": "step-q163004",
  "name": "Question 3",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14184158/assets/1778ff7ea1ee5b1bcf918992d8b2be700362031cd7d142b00b3dc79470983eb0.png)\n\nThe graph above shows a proportional relationship between the time $x$ (in minutes) that water flows through a hose into a tank and the corresponding amount $y$ of water (in gallons) that has entered the tank during that period. What is the unit rate of the water flow?",
  "worked_solution": "The unit rate in gallons per minute is simply the constant of proportionality for the given proportional relationship.\n\nTo compute the constant of proportionality, we first need to choose a point $(x,y)$ on the graph. Here, let's choose the point $(4,15).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14184158/assets/d063a3c2ebf77d1132349b7e48f883632d97720d491a4a5c9158b49c5809258e.png)\n\nNow, we can find the constant of proportionality by computing the ratio of the $y$-coordinate to the $x$-coordinate, as follows:\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{y}{x}=\\frac{15}{4}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the water flow unit rate is $\\frac{15}{4}$ gallons per minute.",
